{"element":{"atomicNumber":14,"symbol":"Si","name":"Silicon","wikidataId":"Q670","wikipediaTitle":"Silicon","wikipediaUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon","period":3,"group":14,"block":"p","category":"metalloid","phase":"solid","mass":28.085,"electronegativity":1.9,"ionizationEnergy":8.152,"radius":210,"summary":"Silicon is a chemical element; it has symbol Si and atomic number 14. It is a hard, brittle crystalline solid with a blue-grey metallic lustre, and is a tetravalent non-metal and semiconductor. It is a member of group 14 in the periodic table: carbon is above it; and germanium, tin, lead, and flerovium are below it. It is relatively unreactive. Silicon is a significant element that is essential for several physiological and metabolic processes in plants. Silicon is widely used as a semiconductor material in various electrical devices such as transistors, solar cells, and integrated circuits. It dominates semiconductor applications due to its low cost, excellent electronic properties, and adaptable physical characteristics.","neighbors":["Al","P"],"rankings":{"mass":105,"electronegativity":36,"ionizationEnergy":33,"radius":46,"density":74,"meltingPoint":33,"boilingPoint":27,"halfLife":0},"sources":{"structured":{"provider":"PubChem","license":"public domain","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/14"},"identifiers":{"provider":"Wikidata","license":"CC0 1.0","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q670"},"summary":{"provider":"Wikipedia","title":"Silicon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","accessDate":"2026-03-30"}},"discoveryYear":1824,"discoverer":"Jöns Jacob Berzelius","etymologyOrigin":"property","etymologyDescription":"Latin silex: flint, hard stone","density":2.3296,"meltingPoint":1687,"boilingPoint":3538,"halfLife":null},"group":{"n":14,"label":"Carbon group","description":"The carbon group ranges from carbon and silicon -- the basis of organic and semiconductor chemistry -- through germanium, tin, lea